{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792507511,"nonce":"pcWJ3hsUukW0ggybNsPmV4vPe+E=","title":"TypeScript: qualify native symbols by module identity","message":"Separate source files currently emit the same ts$fn$name and ts$record$ordinal identities. Shared linking can therefore select another module's body or layout. This blocks native multi-file integration under 0c26889a7cd2c4060eb94ef93fd808c2857089dbf714735ca52b5d071bdb1564.\n\nGive each parsed program an interned module identity, defaulting to its supplied source identity, with an explicit logical identity API for resolvers and library packaging. Qualify functions, lifted arrows, generated bindings and record layouts consistently. Preserve shared runtime extern names, source property names, native generic specialization and direct typed array GEPs. Record the same module origin in the shared registry and initializer/function metadata. Encode identities injectively using linker-safe characters, including punctuation and Unicode identities that LLVM's symbol sanitization could otherwise collapse.\n\nAcceptance: default parsing separates same-basename files; explicit identities stay stable across diagnostic filenames. Link separately parsed and snapshotted libraries with overlapping function/type names and different bodies/layouts, including callbacks and typed arrays. Verify interpreter-first execution and actual Cranelift/LLVM/mixed promotion, static release, unchanged conformance and no new known failures. This closes only symbol identity; TS import/export syntax, resolution and exported checker interfaces remain in the parent module issue.","files":null}]}